course: dictionary
lesson: 3346
title: Spotting exaggeration
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An exaggeration makes something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is. «I'm so hungry I could eat a horse» is an exaggeration, because nobody could really eat a horse. People use exaggeration to be funny or to show strong feelings, and writers use it too.
explain: Exaggeration is also called hyperbole. Ask yourself: could this be true? If it could not be true, you are probably reading an exaggeration. Read the words around it, because they tell you whether the writer is joking or being serious.
explain: «Nothing — absolute nothing — half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats» is a loving exaggeration. The Rat means boats are wonderful, not that nothing else in the world is worth doing. Knowing a few real facts helps you spot when a claim has been stretched too far.
fact: An exaggeration makes something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is.
fact: Exaggeration is the habit or act of exaggerating.
fact: Exaggeration is also called hyperbole, and it is used for humour or effect.
fact: A joke exaggeration that everyone can see is a joke is not a mean lie.
fact: Words like "never" and "always" are often exaggerations of a real feeling.
exercise: «I'm so hungry I could eat a horse.» Is this an exaggeration? | No, it is a list of facts about horses | *Yes, it makes hunger seem bigger than it really is | Yes, it is a true report of a meal | No, it is a question
exercise: Maya says, «We never do anything fun.» What is the best description? | A report of the weather | *An exaggeration of a real feeling | A list of rules | A fact checked by a scientist
exercise: Which word means a statement that makes something seem bigger, better or worse than it really is? | pattern | *exaggeration | lesson | evidence
test: «It's no exaggeration to say the school is huge.» What does this phrase tell you? | The speaker is telling a joke that nobody can see | The speaker has forgotten the facts | *The speaker is stating something true, not stretching it | The speaker is whispering a secret
test: Why is a joke exaggeration that everyone can see is a joke not a mean lie? | Because it is about horses | *Because everyone knows it is not meant to be taken literally | Because it is a fact | Because it is always written down
exam: Which sentence is an exaggeration? | Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level. | The sky looks blue on a sunny day. | *I have told you a million times to tidy your room. | The bus leaves at eight o'clock.
